KERMIT(1)                      P32W Unix                       KERMIT(1)

NAME
       kermit - transfer files over a serial line, Kermit protocol

SYNOPSIS
       kermit [options] -s file ...
       kermit [options] -r [options]
       kermit [options] -x [options]
       kermit [options] -g name ...
       kermit [options] -f [options]

DESCRIPTION
       kermit moves files between P32W and another computer that runs
       a Kermit program, normally C-Kermit on the Mac, connected to
       the same terminal line. It runs on the terminal it was started
       from: it puts that line into raw mode for the transfer and
       restores it when it finishes, so the other Kermit, which was
       just talking to the shell as a terminal emulator, does the
       other side. With -l it uses another line instead.

       Exactly one action is given:

       -s file ...
              Send the files, then end the session. Each file is sent
              under its last path component. Files that do not exist,
              are not regular files or cannot be read are skipped.

       -r     Receive files until the sender ends the session.

       -x     Be a server: wait for commands from the other Kermit
              and carry them out (see SERVER COMMANDS) until it sends
              FINISH or BYE. This is the easy way to work: start
              kermit -x, escape back to C-Kermit and drive everything
              from there.

       -g name ...
              Get files from a Kermit server: ask for each name in
              turn and receive what the server sends. Stops at the
              first name that fails.

       -f     Tell a Kermit server to finish (leave server mode).

       If several actions are given the last one counts. After -s
       or -g every remaining argument is a file name, so all options
       must come before them.

       PROTOCOL
       kermit offers, and uses when the other side agrees: long
       packets (up to 9024 bytes), sliding windows (up to 31), the
       CRC-16 block check (else the 1-character checksum), control
       prefixing with #, 8th-bit prefixing if the other side asks
       for it (the line is 8-bit clean), repeat counts with ~, and
       attribute packets. A damaged or lost packet is sent again,
       up to 10 times. The timeout is 7 seconds until the other side
       states its own; a server waits up to an hour for each command
       and then simply waits again.

       Attributes sent with each file: text or binary, the size in
       KiB and in bytes, the modification time (local time, by TZ)
       and the system type (Unix). Of received attributes, kermit
       uses the text/binary type and the modification time, which
       it sets on the file; it never refuses a file.

       RECEIVED FILES
       Files are created in the current directory, mode 0644 less
       the umask, replacing any file of that name. Only the last
       component of the incoming name is used; a name in all capital
       letters is changed to lower case, and names are cut to 28
       characters, the longest P32W allows. A file the sender
       discards in the middle of a transfer is removed.

       USING C-KERMIT ON THE MAC
       Install it with brew install c-kermit, then connect it to a
       P32W terminal line.

       In the emulator (started with p32emu ... -T 5000, which puts
       the second terminal, tty1, on TCP port 5000):

              set host /raw-socket localhost 5000
              connect

       On the hardware, with a USB serial adapter:

              set line /dev/cu.usbserial-...
              set speed 115200
              set flow none
              set carrier-watch off
              connect

       Log in, type kermit -x, then escape back to C-Kermit with
       Ctrl-\ followed by c. Now use send, get, remote dir, remote
       cd, remote delete, remote type and finish at the C-Kermit
       prompt. C-Kermit decides text or binary by the file name and
       says so in its attribute packet, so text files arrive with LF
       line ends without -T. After finish, connect again to get
       back to the shell.

       On the Mac, tools/pykermit.py --tcp 5000 send|get|dir|finish
       ... in the P32W source tree does the same against a P32W server
       without C-Kermit.

OPTIONS
       Each option is a separate argument (-T -q, not -Tq); only its
       first letter is looked at.

       -a name
              Send or receive as name. When sending, it applies only
              if a single file is sent; when receiving, every
              incoming file is written to this one name.

       -T     Text mode. Sent files go out with CR LF line ends and
              are announced as text; received files are taken as
              text (CR LF becomes LF) unless the sender's attribute
              packet says binary. Without -T, files are sent as
              binary, and received ones are binary unless the sender
              says text.

       -e length
              The longest packet to send or accept, 40 to 9024
              (default 1000). The other side's limit also applies.

       -w window
              The largest sliding window, 1 to 31 packets (default
              3). The smaller of this and the other side's offer is
              used.

       -l device
              Use device (for example /dev/tty1) as the line instead
              of the standard input and output.

       -q     Quiet: no progress messages. Messages are only ever
              written with -l, since on the controlling terminal
              they would corrupt the packets; so -q matters only
              together with -l.

       A missing argument after -a, -e, -w or -l is ignored. An
       unknown option prints a usage message.

SERVER COMMANDS
       A kermit -x server understands these requests, named here as
       C-Kermit spells them:

       send file ...
              Receive the files (wildcards are expanded by C-Kermit).

       get name
              Send the named file. name may contain * and ? in its
              last component (dir/*.c); files whose names start with
              . are left out, and at most 256 files are sent. Each
              goes under its last path component.

       remote dir [dir]
              A listing of the directory (default the current one):
              one line per entry, d or -, the size, the name. Names
              starting with . are left out.

       remote cd [dir]
              Change the server's directory; with no dir, to /.

       remote delete name
              Remove one file (no wildcards).

       remote type name
              Show the file (its first 16 KiB).

       remote help
              A one-line summary of what the server supports.

       finish, bye
              End the server; kermit exits and the shell prompt
              returns. bye does not log out.

       Other requests (remote space, remote who, remote host and so
       on) are answered with "not implemented". Short replies come
       back in the acknowledgement, longer ones as text that C-Kermit
       shows on its screen.

EXAMPLES
       kermit -x                     be a server; drive it from C-Kermit
       kermit -s notes.txt           send one file (C-Kermit: receive)
       kermit -T -s memo             send a text file with CR LF ends
       kermit -a old.c -s new.c      send new.c under the name old.c
       kermit -r                     receive (C-Kermit: send file)
       kermit -g prog.c              get prog.c from a server
       kermit -l /dev/tty1 -r        receive on the other terminal

       A session with C-Kermit, after kermit -x on P32W:

              C-Kermit> send hello.c
              C-Kermit> remote cd /usr/ken
              C-Kermit> get *.c
              C-Kermit> remote dir
              C-Kermit> finish

EXIT STATUS
       0 if the transfer (or the server session) ended normally;
       1 if it failed (too many retries, an error packet from the
       other side, a file that could not be created), if the device
       of -l could not be opened, or if kermit was stopped by a
       signal; 2 for a usage error, including -s with no files.

SEE ALSO
       stty(1), tar(1), gzip(1), zip(1), unzip(1)

BUGS
       Options cannot be combined in one argument, and must come
       before -s or -g.

       There are no interactive commands, no CONNECT mode and no
       script language; kermit is a command-line transfer program
       only. As a client it can only send, receive, get and finish:
       it cannot ask a server for remote dir, cd and the like.

       Received files do not keep a mode; the size attribute is not
       checked, and a full disk is not noticed (write errors are
       ignored). An existing file is replaced without warning.

       A server sends a file only if its path, as typed or as found
       by a wildcard (./name or dir/name), is shorter than 32
       characters; longer ones are left out without a message.
       Wildcards are allowed only in the last component.

       remote type shows at most 16 KiB; remote dir stops after
       about 16 KiB of listing. remote delete takes one file.

       The 2-character block check is accepted on incoming packets
       but never negotiated: it is CRC-16 or the plain checksum.

       Kermit dates carry no zone. kermit reads and writes them as
       local time by TZ, so the two machines' zones must agree for
       the times to come out right.
